y = 65\\) into slope-intercept form \\(y = mx + b\\), where \\(m\\) is the slope.\n\nStarting with:\n\\[ 3x - 7y = 65 \\]\n\nIsolate \\(y\\):\n\\[ -7y = -3x + 65 \\]\n\\[ y = \\frac{3}{7}x - \\frac{65}{7} \\]\n\nFrom this, we see that the slope \\(m\\) of the given line is:\n\\[ m = \\frac{3}{7} \\]\n\n### Step 2: Use the slope for the parallel line\nSince the line we are looking for is parallel to the given line, it must have the same slope. Therefore, the slope of the line passing through \\((7,4)\\) and \\((0,K)\\) is also \\(\\frac{3}{7}\\).\n\n### Step 3: Use the slope formula to find \\(K\\)\nThe slope \\(m\\) of a line passing through two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is given by:\n\\[ m = \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1} \\]\n\nSubstitute the points \\((7,4)\\) and \\((0,K)\\) into the slope formula:\n\\[ \\frac{K - 4}{0 - 7} = \\frac{3}{7} \\]\n\nSimplify the left side:\n\\[ \\frac{K - 4}{-7} = \\frac{3}{7} \\]\n\n### Step 4: Solve for \\(K\\)\nTo solve for \\(K\\), we can cross-multiply:\n\\[ K - 4 = 3 \\times (-1) \\]\n\\[ K - 4 = -3 \\]\n\nAdd 4 to both sides:\n\\[ K = 1 \\]\n\n### Final Answer\nThe value of \\( K \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{1} \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{1}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:135"}
